sec.p:
Significantly, in all cases, the reliability of annotations can be considerably increased by examining the relative position of the candidates in the ranked list (Fig. 4d-f). As mentioned, we score candidates based on the cosine similarity between the target empirical spectrum and the in silico predicted spectrum for each candidate. However, the difference between the scores of the first and second candidates is particularly informative about the reliability of the top candidate. When the two scores are very close, the top candidate is the true metabolite in 20%–30% of cases (Fig. 4d–f), consistent with the overall figures in Fig. 4a–c. However, when there is a large gap between the cosine similarities of the first and second candidates, the top candidate is much more likely to be the true molecule. Specifically, when the difference in cosine similarity scores (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
