$f_{\mathrm{m/z}} N_{\mathrm{val}}$\end{document} within the validation set (rounded upwards to the next integer). Note that the threshold value depends on the modeling approach (ANN, GNN, and CNN) and the m/z bin we consider. By binarizing scores in this way, we ensure that our models are statistically calibrated.

list-item.p:
Precision: Measures the proportion of true positive predictions among all positive predictions made by the model. It indicates how many of the predicted positives are actually correct. (1)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
\begin{align*}& \mathrm{Precision} = \frac{TP}{TP + FP}\end{align*}\end{document}

Recall: Also known as sensitivity, it measures the proportion of true positive predictions among all actual positives. It indicates how well the model can identify positive instances. (2)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
\begin{align*}& \mathrm{Recall} = \frac{TP}{TP + FN}\end{align*}\end{document}

F1: Combines Precision and Recall into a single metric by taking their harmonic mean. It provides a balance between Precision and Recall, especially useful when there is an uneven class distribution. (3)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
\begin{align*}& F1 = 2 \cdot{\frac{\mathrm{Precision} \cdot \mathrm{Recall}}{\mathrm{Precision} + \mathrm{Recall}}}\end{align*}\end{document}

Accuracy: Measures the proportion of all correct predictions (both true positives and true negatives) among the total number of cases evaluated. It indicates the overall effectiveness of the model. (4)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
\begin{align*}& \mathrm{Accuracy} = \frac{TP + TN}{TP + TN + FP + FN}\end{align*}\end{document}

Cosine Similarity: Measures the cosine of the angle between two vectors in an inner product space. It quantifies how similar the vectors are by determining if they point in approximately the same direction. (5)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
\begin{align*}& \cos(A, B) = \frac{A \cdot B}{\left\lVert A \right\lVert \, \left\lVert B \right\lVert}\end{align*}\end{document}
